In either setting <span class="upright">(i)</span> or <span class="upright">(ii)</span>, the enhancement $\mathfrak B$ induces a perfect obstruction theory $E_B\to L_B$, with virtual tangent $$\label{eq:quot-tangent}
E_B^\vee\simeq
\mathop{\mathrm{fib}}\left\{
R\!\mathop{\mathrm{Hom}}(S',Q)\xrightarrow{d\det^o}
R\Gamma(\mathcal I_o)[1]\otimes\mathcal O_B
\right\}.$$ It is $G$-equivariant.

On $X^{[r]}$, with universal ideal $\mathcal I$, there is a quasi-isomorphism $$\label{fix:hilbert-splitting}
 R\!\mathop{\mathrm{Hom}}(\mathcal I,\mathcal I)
 \simeq R\Gamma(\mathcal O_X)\oplus T_{X^{[r]}}[-1].$$ The two maps to the right-hand side are the trace and the inverse of the trace-free Kodaira–Spencer isomorphism.

For the projection $f:W\to U_b$ to the unrestricted low rank-one Quot, there is a relative perfect obstruction theory whose virtual tangent is $$\label{fix:relative-tangent}
 T_f^\mathrm{vir}=
 \mathop{\mathrm{fib}}\!\left\{R\!\mathop{\mathrm{Hom}}(S'_a,Q_a)
       \longrightarrow R\Gamma(\mathcal I_o)[1]\right\}.$$ It is compatible with the absolute theories of $W$ and $U_b$, and $$\label{fix:relative-class}
 [W]^\mathrm{vir}=f^![U_b]^\mathrm{vir}.$$

Every such fixed contribution with $\alpha H\ne0$ satisfies $$\label{nr:survivors}
 KH=H^2=k+1,\qquad
 n_1=n_2=0,\qquad m_H=0,\qquad m_T=p.$$

For the common universal coefficients defined above, one has $$\label{r1:comparison}
 c_{\mathrm{out}}-s c_{\mathrm{mid}}=0,
 \qquad s>0,$$ where $-s$ is the canonical-divisor coefficient in Equation <a href="#eq:canonical-cycle" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:canonical-cycle">[eq:canonical-cycle]</a> for $Y$.

