(Lk^*E_B)^{\mathrm{fix}}\xrightarrow{\sim}E_W,$$ compatible with their maps to $L_W$. Its virtual tangent is $$\label{der:fixed-tangent}
E_W^\vee\simeq
\mathop{\mathrm{fib}}\left\{
\bigoplus_{i=1}^2R\!\mathop{\mathrm{Hom}}(S'_i,Q_i)
\longrightarrow R\Gamma(\mathcal I_o)[1]\otimes\mathcal O_W
\right\}.$$ The moving virtual normal complex is $$\label{der:moving-normal}
N_W^{\mathrm{vir}}=\bigoplus_{i\ne j}R\!\mathop{\mathrm{Hom}}(S'_i,Q_j),$$ where the $(i,j)$ summand has the difference between the characters on $L_j$ and $L_i$, or evaluated weight $w_j-w_i$.

Let $R$ be a noetherian parameter scheme and let $L_R\twoheadrightarrow Q$ be a flat family of rank-zero quotients of a line bundle on $X\times R$. Its kernel has a unique factorization $$S'=L_R(-\mathcal C)\mathcal I_Z,$$ where $\mathcal C$ is a relative effective Cartier divisor and $Z\subset X\times R$ is finite and flat over $R$. Both factors commute with arbitrary base change. Consequently, the rank-one Quot functor with specified divisor component and point length $r$ is represented by $$\label{fix:rank-one-product}
 U=\mathop{\mathrm{Div}}^P(X)\times X^{[r]},
 \qquad S'=L(-\mathcal C)\mathcal I_Z.$$ The assertion includes nonreduced parameter schemes.

On $X^{[r]}$, with universal ideal $\mathcal I$, there is a quasi-isomorphism $$\label{fix:hilbert-splitting}
 R\!\mathop{\mathrm{Hom}}(\mathcal I,\mathcal I)
 \simeq R\Gamma(\mathcal O_X)\oplus T_{X^{[r]}}[-1].$$ The two maps to the right-hand side are the trace and the inverse of the trace-free Kodaira–Spencer isomorphism. On $\mathop{\mathrm{Div}}^P(X)\times X^{[r]}$, define $$\label{fix:point-bundle}
 F=\bigl(\pi_*\mathcal O_Z(K-\mathcal C)\bigr)^\vee.$$ It is a vector bundle of rank $r$, and relative Serre duality gives $$\label{fix:point-duality}
 R\!\mathop{\mathrm{Hom}}(\mathcal O_Z,\mathcal O(\mathcal C))=F[-2].$$

For the projection $f:W\to U_b$ to the unrestricted low rank-one Quot, there is a relative perfect obstruction theory whose virtual tangent is $$\label{fix:relative-tangent}
 T_f^\mathrm{vir}=
 \mathop{\mathrm{fib}}\!\left\{R\!\mathop{\mathrm{Hom}}(S'_a,Q_a)
       \longrightarrow R\Gamma(\mathcal I_o)[1]\right\}.$$ It is compatible with the absolute theories of $W$ and $U_b$, and $$\label{fix:relative-class}
 [W]^\mathrm{vir}=f^![U_b]^\mathrm{vir}.$$

Every such fixed contribution with $\alpha H\ne0$ satisfies $$\label{nr:survivors}
 KH=H^2=k+1,\qquad
 n_1=n_2=0,\qquad m_H=0,\qquad m_T=p.$$

